/*
  Psychiatric Interviewing Trainer — Study guide, Chapter 9: Mood Disorders
  Written in our own words as a companion to Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing: The Art of Understanding (3rd ed.).
  Clinical exchanges are composed for teaching; they are not transcripts from the book. Book cases are summarized.
  Schema: see guide-ch1.js.
*/
window.TRAINER = window.TRAINER || { parts: {} };
window.TRAINER.guides = window.TRAINER.guides || {};
window.TRAINER.guides.ch9 = {
  chapter: "ch9",
  intro: "The first chapter of Part II teaches differential diagnosis the way Shea wants it done: as part of the art of understanding, never as a checklist. It opens with a short, friendly course on how diagnostic systems are built (and therefore where they fail), then walks through five people who all look depressed and turn out to need five different formulations. Along the way you learn the everyday tasks that every initial assessment needs: a history of the presenting disorder, a past psychiatric history, a family history, and the habit of always asking about mania and always thinking organic. Read it before your first week of admissions; the practice items and the two Chapter 9 simulations are built directly on it.",

  highYield: {
    mustKnow: [
      "No diagnostic system is perfect. [[Validity / reliability|Validity]] (does it describe the person accurately?) and reliability (do two interviewers reach the same answer, in the time available?) pull against each other. A system used without knowing its limits can do harm.",
      "The DSM-5 stays [[Categorical vs dimensional systems|categorical]] for ease of use but adds dimensional [[Specifier|specifiers]] (anxious distress, mixed features, melancholic, atypical, psychotic, catatonia, peripartum onset, seasonal, remission, severity). Shea calls this one of its biggest advances, and a diagnosis with high [[Descriptive essence]] is one whose name and criteria call its typical patient to mind.",
      "Criterion A of a major depressive episode needs five of nine symptoms over two weeks, and one of them must be depressed mood or [[Anhedonia]]. A patient can meet criteria without ever saying the word depressed. [[SIG E CAPS]] holds the list.",
      "[[Neurovegetative symptoms]] (sleep, appetite and weight, energy, libido, concentration, motor change) are the hallmarks of major depression and are woven into conversation with [[Stilted / blended expansion|blended expansions]], never fired as a checklist. Asked well, they engage: the patient feels understood and senses that you know this illness.",
      "[[Early morning awakening]] has a phenomenology: jolted awake by a stream of frets, unable to get back to sleep, dreading the day, often puzzled that the worries seem to have grown overnight. Severe forms, with mornings worst, mark [[Melancholic features|melancholia]].",
      "The bipolar trap: a severely depressed patient and an empathically absorbed interviewer together produce no contextual cue to ask about mania. Ask every depressed patient about current and past manic and hypomanic symptoms, and expect past episodes to be [[Sealing over|sealed over]]; family may remember what the patient cannot or will not.",
      "Mixed states are common, not rare. In the DSM-5 a full depressive or manic episode plus three symptoms of the opposite pole earns [[Mixed features|with mixed features]]. Shea's proposed subtype, [[Dysphoric mania]], is easily mistaken for an agitated depression or for a budding personality disorder, especially in adolescents and young adults.",
      "Three tips that a 'depression' may be a dysphoric mania: persistent anger as a leading symptom, an angst of unusual bitter intensity, and a mismatch between that angst and surprisingly mild neurovegetative symptoms. Driven to act rather than helpless; focused outward on the wrongs of the world rather than inward on one's own inadequacy.",
      "Before any antidepressant, hunt for three things: current hypomanic signs, a past hypomanic or manic episode, and a family history of bipolar process (including the relatives who were 'just nutty' or had a 'motor mouth'). [[Bipolar II disorder]], [[Substance/medication-induced bipolar disorder]], and [[Cyclothymic disorder]] are all easy to miss and all change treatment.",
      "Up to about a third of people who meet criteria for major depression deny being depressed. Switch words (sad, unhappy, not yourself, not up to par), ask when they last felt like crying, listen for cultural idioms such as [[Nervios]], and use collateral informants.",
      "[[Atypical features]] are not atypical: [[Mood reactivity]] plus two of increased appetite or weight, hypersomnia, [[Leaden paralysis]], and long-standing [[Rejection sensitivity]]. Their presence should prompt a harder search for bipolar process.",
      "Always think organic. A good reason to be depressed (a death, a job loss, a divorce) does not rule out a concurrent medical cause. Ask about prescription and over-the-counter medications, and remember Anderson's six categories.",
      "'I've been depressed for years' usually predicts that a sustained major depressive episode is not present. Test persistence: a full two weeks, nearly every day, with little fluctuation. Fluctuating mood points toward [[Persistent depressive disorder]], cyclothymia, personality disorders, substances, adjustment disorders, or [[V-codes]].",
      "A family history taken with one blanket question yields false negatives. Explain why you are asking, name blood relatives explicitly, say alcohol and drugs out loud, define technical terms, ask about hospitalizations and suicide separately, and adapt the wording to the patient's culture."
    ],
    pitfalls: [
      "Memorizing hundreds of criteria instead of building a working knowledge of what clarifies the major diagnoses, and then interviewing from the page rather than from the person.",
      "Announcing 'I need to ask a few questions now' and running the neurovegetative symptoms as a list.",
      "Asking about sex drive apologetically, or failing to clarify that you mean interest, not activity, so the single patient answers 'none, I'm not seeing anyone.'",
      "Taking a depressed patient's denial of past mania at face value, and never calling the mother, spouse, or sibling who would have described the week without sleep.",
      "Reading a happy mania as safe. Euphoric patients can turn hostile within seconds when redirected or limited, especially in the emergency department and on the unit.",
      "Diagnosing a depressed, irritable, self-cutting 18-year-old with borderline personality disorder (or a major depression) without searching for racing thoughts, drivenness, dark imagery, a family history of bipolar process, and prior reactions to antidepressants.",
      "Starting an antidepressant before asking about hypomania, and then reading the new agitation, insomnia, and strange suicidal urges as a worsening depression rather than an unleashing.",
      "The opposite error: labeling a child or adolescent with angry outbursts as bipolar when the picture is stress, ADHD, oppositional behavior, or a depressive equivalent ([[DMDD]] exists to prevent exactly this).",
      "Accepting the first onset date the patient offers; it is usually when things got bad, weeks or months after the illness began.",
      "Forgetting that a patient on partial treatment may look sub-threshold. Ask what the symptoms were just before the medication started.",
      "Assuming psychological causality because the stressor is obvious, and skipping the medication list, the thyroid, and the rest of the organic screen.",
      "Writing 'no personality disorder' nowhere, so the next reader of the record cannot tell whether anyone looked."
    ],
    phrases: [
      { say: "Over the last few weeks, have you still felt like doing the things you told me you enjoy, or has something changed?", when: "Exploring anhedonia after first learning the patient's interests (the [[Wellness triad|wellness triad]] groundwork). Composed." },
      { say: "Some people with depression find they wake too early, with worries already running, and can't get back to sleep. Does that happen to you?", when: "Screening for early morning awakening by describing its phenomenology instead of asking only about wake time. Composed." },
      { say: "Depression tends to dampen basic drives like appetite and sexual interest. Has it affected your interest in sex? I mean interest, not whether you're actually having it.", when: "Libido, asked matter-of-factly in a natural context, with the normalizing reason attached. Composed." },
      { say: "Has there ever been a stretch of days or weeks when you felt the opposite of this: much more energy than usual, little need for sleep, ready to take on anything, maybe even right after a low period?", when: "The question every depressed patient should hear, whatever the context fails to suggest. Composed." },
      { say: "Do you ever get spells, even just hours, when you suddenly feel unusually up and wired for no reason you can name, and it strikes you as a little odd?", when: "Screening for euphoric hypomania. The 'odd to you' clause reduces false positives from ordinary good days. Composed." },
      { say: "And spells when your thoughts suddenly race in an unpleasant way you can't control, everything turns angrier and darker, and again you can't explain why?", when: "Follow-up screen for dysphoric hypomanic bursts. Composed." },
      { say: "Do you feel you have to do something about this, and do it now, even if part of you knows it isn't wise?", when: "Testing for the drivenness of a dysphoric mania against the helplessness of an agitated depression. Composed." },
      { say: "When was the last time you felt like crying?", when: "A [[Gentle assumption|gentle assumption]] for the patient, often male, who denies both depression and sadness; it asks only about the urge, so there is room to save face." },
      { say: "Some people's low mood stays with them day after day; for others it comes and goes like a roller coaster. Where would you put yourself?", when: "Testing persistence when the patient says 'depressed for years.' Composed." },
      { say: "Think back to the months before that. Were you completely your usual self, or already not quite right?", when: "Pushing the onset date earlier after the first answer, paired with an [[Anchor question|anchor question]] tied to a holiday or family event. Composed." },
      { say: "Has anyone in your family, including grandparents, aunts, uncles, and cousins, had trouble like yours, or ever been in hospital for their nerves? Has anyone ever tried to end their life?", when: "A family history that names the relatives, uses lay words, and asks about suicide as its own question. Composed." }
    ]
  },

  sections: [
    {
      id: "systems",
      title: "Diagnostic systems: there has never been a perfect one",
      lede: "A short course on validity, reliability, categories, dimensions, and why it matters at the bedside.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Shea opens with Blake walking London's streets and reading sorrow in every face, then with two women of his own: an unemployed designer whose days had shrunk to compartments of anxiety and self-doubt, and a bereaved daughter found wandering a cemetery with a knife because her dead father's voice was calling her. Both were depressed. Mood disorders are not things but processes, lived differently by each person and yet similar enough to be recognized. Sensitive differential diagnosis therefore needs understanding on many levels at once, and in Shea's model it is never separated from the search for the person beneath the diagnosis. Patients accept help from clinicians they feel are trying to understand them." },
        { type: "p", text: "Before any criteria, the chapter asks you to understand how diagnostic systems are designed, because the design creates their limits. [[Validity / reliability|Validity]] is a system's ability to describe accurately the person or phenomenon in front of you. The catch is that the most valid system may be too long or too complex to use in a real clinic. Reliability, specifically inter-rater reliability, is the property that different interviewers seeing the same patient reach the same diagnosis in a timely way; without it, clinicians cannot talk to one another and research stops. The two pull against each other: counting every tree (validity) versus naming the forest quickly (reliability). Neither can be maximized at once, so the DSM-5 and the ICD are both compromises." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Construct validity: the system rests on accepted clinical principles and the best available evidence, and its parts follow logically from one another.",
          "Face validity: on the face of it, the categories and their criteria fit how patients with these disorders actually present.",
          "[[Descriptive essence]] (Shea's term, a special aspect of face validity): the key hallmarks jump out as you scan the criteria, and the name of the disorder itself calls up the typical patient. High descriptive essence makes criteria easy to remember, which makes them easy to ask about."
        ] },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Categorical systems", items: [
            "Discrete categories, qualitatively different from one another (depressive disorders, bipolar disorders, anxiety disorders, personality disorders).",
            "Each diagnosis has a criteria set: for major depression, five or more of nine symptoms.",
            "Specific, memorable criteria mean faster diagnosis and higher agreement between clinicians.",
            "Hard to design well; when they fail, validity is what suffers."
          ] },
          b: { title: "Dimensional systems", items: [
            "Symptoms, behaviors, and traits shade into one another; forcing them into boxes is artificial.",
            "Everyone shows a trait to some degree; the question is how intense, how frequent, and how variable across time and situation (anger from appropriate to the rage of a dysphoric mania).",
            "The more characteristics surveyed, the more accurate the portrait, and the more valid the system.",
            "Can be unwieldy and impractical in a first interview."
          ] }
        },
        { type: "p", text: "The DSM-5 remains primarily categorical but added dimensional qualities, which Shea considers one of its most significant advances over the DSM-IV-TR, and it even offers a hybrid model for personality disorders. The main tool is the expanded [[Specifier]]. His illustration is insight in OCD: the DSM-IV-TR could not distinguish the patient who knows the hand washing is excessive from the one convinced that germs will kill her, and the DSM-5 can (good or fair, poor, absent or delusional), with obvious consequences for treatment and for engaging the patient in it. For depressive disorders the specifiers include anxious distress (with a severity dimension), mixed features, melancholic features, atypical features, psychotic features, catatonia, peripartum onset, seasonal pattern, remission status, and severity. Learn them; they let you describe what the patient is experiencing and communicate it to colleagues, and in this chapter dimensionality turns out to be the key to recognizing bipolar disorder in its many forms." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Working knowledge, not memorization", text: "Accurate diagnosis requires familiarity with the DSM-5, which means knowing what material clarifies the major diagnoses, not reciting hundreds of criteria. That frees your attention for eliciting the material while keeping the patient engaged. The alliance stays paramount." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "quiz", id: "ch9", label: "Quiz: Chapter 9" }]
    },

    {
      id: "criteria",
      title: "Major depression and persistent depressive disorder",
      lede: "The two common depressive diagnoses, and a mnemonic that fits on a prescription pad.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The chapter's clinical presentations assume about forty minutes of interview have passed. Everyone described is in pain and everyone looks depressed; the work is deciding whether each has a major depressive disorder, a bipolar disorder, a persistent depressive disorder, or something else. Here is what you need to carry in your head." },
        { type: "table", head: ["", "Major depressive episode", "Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia)"], rows: [
          ["Core requirement", "Five or more of nine symptoms in the same two weeks, a change from before, with at least one being depressed mood or loss of interest or pleasure", "Depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, for at least two years (one year in children and adolescents, where the mood may be irritable)"],
          ["Symptom list", "Depressed mood (irritable in youth); diminished interest or pleasure; appetite or weight change; insomnia or hypersomnia; observable psychomotor agitation or retardation; fatigue; worthlessness or excessive guilt; poor concentration or indecision; recurrent thoughts of death or suicidal ideation, plan, or attempt", "While depressed, two or more of: appetite change, sleep change, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration or indecision, hopelessness"],
          ["Course", "Symptoms nearly every day for the two weeks", "Never symptom-free for more than two months in the two years; a major depressive episode may be continuously present"],
          ["Exclusions", "Not due to a substance or medical condition; not better explained by a psychotic disorder; never a manic or hypomanic episode (unless all were substance- or illness-induced)", "Never a manic or hypomanic episode, never cyclothymia; not a psychotic disorder; not a substance or medical condition such as hypothyroidism"],
          ["Both", "Clinically significant distress or impairment", "Clinically significant distress or impairment"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Two notes from the criteria deserve emphasis. First, responses to a major loss (bereavement, financial ruin, disaster, serious illness) can resemble an episode; the DSM-5 asks you to use clinical judgment, informed by the person's history and cultural norms, about whether a major depressive episode is present in addition to the normal response. Second, the dysthymia criteria consolidate what the DSM-IV called chronic major depression and dysthymic disorder, so a patient who has met full episode criteria during the current chronic illness is given major depressive disorder." },
        { type: "p", text: "To keep the nine symptoms available mid-conversation, Shea passes on Cary Gross's mnemonic, popularized by Carlat: [[SIG E CAPS]], a 'prescription' for remembering depression. Sleep change, Interest deficit, Guilt (with worthlessness and hopelessness), Energy deficit, Concentration deficit, Appetite change, Psychomotor change, Suicidality. Prescribers remember it because SIG is on every pad; everyone else remembers it because it is odd." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "drill", id: "mood-pictures", label: "Drill: Mood presentations" }]
    },

    {
      id: "exploring",
      title: "Exploring anhedonia and the neurovegetative symptoms",
      lede: "Mr. Evans, part one: how to ask about pleasure, sleep, appetite, energy, and sex drive so that the asking itself engages.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Mr. Evans", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A 61-year-old man, retired a year from a senior administrative post with the police, arrives with the fiancée who hopes to open a bar with him. He is neatly dressed, speaks softly and slowly as if thinking costs effort, studies the floor, says it is strange that he cannot cry, and reports that nothing gives him pleasure, that sleep, appetite, libido, and energy are gone, and that he sees no future. In the past three weeks he has several times held a loaded revolver to his head. The fiancée, concerned and a little irritated, says nothing cheers him up and that she now has to face the licensing board alone." },
        { type: "p", text: "Major depression is common (about 7% of the United States population in a given year), roughly one and a half to three times more frequent in females from adolescence on, three times more frequent in 18- to 29-year-olds than in people over 60, and lethal: up to 15% of patients die by suicide. Mr. Evans reports depressed mood, but remember that he did not need to. Criterion A is satisfied by either depressed mood or [[Anhedonia]], the markedly diminished interest or pleasure of symptom A-2, a decreased ability to experience, anticipate, or take interest in pleasure. Some patients with full-blown episodes never say the word depressed." },
        { type: "p", text: "Shea's method for anhedonia begins before the symptom. First learn what the person enjoys, the [[Wellness triad|wellness triad]] groundwork from Chapter 6: time away from work, hobbies and sports, reading or screens, the web and social media, socializing, and whether work itself was ever enjoyable. This is worth real time, because interests reveal how the person is put together. In his example a patient's taste for heavy theological reading, mentioned with a tiny smile and a 'used to be fairly religious' that trailed off, opened the spiritual wing of the matrix and a possible source of either torment or healing. Only then come the anhedonia questions: over the past several weeks, has he felt like doing these things, and are they as enjoyable as before? Interpersonal questions work too; a grandfather who loves his grandchildren but cannot enjoy even them has told you what you needed to know." },
        { type: "p", text: "[[Neurovegetative symptoms]] have no standard definition; Shea uses the term for symptoms suggesting that basic regulatory physiology is disturbed: appetite, weight, sleep, energy, libido, concentration, and retarded or agitated motor activity, with constipation, dry mouth, and cold extremities as frequent companions. They are the classic hallmarks of a major depressive episode and must be actively sought if not volunteered. Done properly, the questions show the patient two reassuring things: that you are interested in a whole person whose illness touches every part of life, and that you know this illness, because the questions land." },
        { type: "p", text: "Sleep deserves a thorough exploration of its own. [[Early morning awakening]] is more than waking earlier than wanted. The patient is abruptly awakened by a steady stream of unpleasant worries; as one fret fades another arrives; the day's problems feel insurmountable; getting back to sleep is nearly impossible despite staying in bed; and, curiously, the person went to bed feeling better and cannot understand why the worries grew overnight. Shea quotes a physician patient for whom the sensation was closer to fear than worry and who, oddly, felt better as the morning went on. Milder versions appear in mild and moderate episodes; severe versions, with mornings the worst time of day, belong to melancholia. Ask about it by describing it. Then cover the rest: sleep continuity problems (also seen in psychosis, substance use, and older age), trouble falling asleep (depression, mania, anxiety, substances, adjustment, psychosis), increased or daytime sleep, a reversed day-night pattern (depression, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia), and the incidental discovery of sleep apnea, narcolepsy, or nocturnal myoclonus." },
        { type: "p", text: "Libido makes interviewers shy, and it need not. Raise it in a natural context: after a run of neurovegetative questions ('your depression has really upset your system; has it affected your sex drive too?') or when the patient has been describing strain in a relationship. Ask matter-of-factly, without hesitation. Add the reason, that depression commonly lowers basic drives like appetite and sexual desire, and watch relief arrive; patients have told Shea they thought the loss was one more personal failure. Clarify that you mean interest, not activity, or the patient who is not dating will report no libido when it is in fact intact. And if the reaction is unusually strong, you have learned something about the patient's views of sex, body, or disclosure for later." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "Weaving the symptoms in", lines: [
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Everything feels like too much. My hours got cut, and now my wife is on my case about money." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "You're carrying a lot right now. Is it getting into your sleep at all?", tag: "empathic statement, then the first neurovegetative question" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "I wake up at four or five and that's it. I just lie there." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Do you wake naturally, or do the worries sort of jolt you awake?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Jolt. Work, the kids, the mortgage. One after another." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "That sounds exhausting. And falling asleep at the start of the night, how long does that take?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "That part's fine. Twenty minutes, maybe." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "With that little sleep, I'd wonder about your energy during the day." },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Gone. Mowing the lawn feels like climbing a mountain. People probably think I'm lazy." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Losing interest and energy is part of depression; it isn't laziness. Sometimes appetite goes with it. Has yours changed?", tag: "guilt assuaged, next symptom introduced" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Food doesn't taste like anything. I skip meals." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Are your clothes fitting more loosely?" }
        ], note: "Composed. Each symptom follows from the last through an empathic statement or a bridging remark, the [[Stilted / blended expansion|blended expansion]] of Chapter 4, so the patient experiences a conversation about his life rather than an inventory. Note the concrete follow-ups (how many awakenings, what time, how long to fall asleep, loose clothes, over how many months) that turn a yes into data." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Why this matters beyond the diagnosis", text: "By the end of a well-done neurovegetative exploration you hold a vivid picture of what depression feels like for this person, and the person feels that you are one step closer to understanding. The same questions, asked as a list after 'I need to ask a few things now,' collect the same data and lose the alliance." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c9p2", label: "Practice: exploring anhedonia" }, { kind: "practice", id: "c9p3", label: "Practice: asking about libido" }]
    },

    {
      id: "melancholia-anxiety",
      title: "Melancholia, anxiety, alcohol, and the past psychiatric history",
      lede: "The severe variant, the specifier that doubles as a suicide probe, the substance that mimics depression, and the history that must be taken.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Mr. Evans's anhedonia and neurovegetative symptoms are severe enough to meet the specifier [[Melancholic features|with melancholic features]], a particularly severe depression more frequent in older patients. It requires loss of pleasure in all or almost all activities and/or no lift, even briefly, when something good happens (the fiancée's 'nothing cheers him up'), plus three of six: a distinctly unpleasant quality of mood, depression regularly worse in the morning, early morning awakening at least two hours before the usual time, marked psychomotor agitation or retardation, significant anorexia or weight loss, and excessive or inappropriate guilt. The mood deserves description. It may be profound despondency to the point of despair, or moroseness, or what Shea calls an empty mood: no connection to life, 'I care about nothing,' an abandoned house with no light in it. Such patients can stare past you as if you were not in the room; to them, you are not." },
        { type: "p", text: "Older systems kept mood and anxiety disorders apart; experience says they overlap heavily, and some 'depressive' symptoms such as agitation and early morning awakening are experienced by patients as anxious. Screen every depressed patient for anxiety, and not only for completeness. Jan Fawcett's research and repeated warnings: intense anxiety in a depressive state raises suicide risk, panic attacks raise it further, and prolonged or intense anxiety combined with impulsivity, as when manic features are part of the picture or in a mixed state, makes a patient especially dangerous to themselves. The DSM-5 anxious distress specifier, rated mild to severe, is therefore not a nicety but a sensitive probe toward a better estimate of suicide risk." },
        { type: "p", text: "The plan to open a bar is a clue. Mr. Evans had past alcohol dependence with social dysfunction and has been sober for about a year. Alcohol and drugs travel with depression in three arrangements: drinking can cause depressive symptoms (most clear after detoxification, but they can last two months, and sleep disturbance six), depression can precede drinking as self-medication, or the two can coincide. When heavy current drinking is reported, treat major depression as a tentative or rule-out diagnosis; many clinicians wait for weeks of sustained abstinence before confirming it, while clear depressive symptoms predating the drinking make a true major depression more likely. No survey of depressive symptoms is complete without a thorough history of current and past substance use." },
        { type: "p", text: "His case also shows the value of a past psychiatric history, which should be actively elicited as time allows (what is missed goes to the next interview or the receiving team):" },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Past diagnoses; in a depressed patient, search specifically for prior depression, mania, or hypomania.",
          "Hospitalizations (where and when) and outpatient treatment (with whom).",
          "Medications: names, doses, duration, whether the patient liked them, and side effects.",
          "Psychotherapy: with whom, when, what it consisted of, and the patient's opinion of it.",
          "Electroconvulsive therapy, light therapy, and alternative interventions such as St. John's wort, acupuncture, or meditation.",
          "Current psychotherapy (current medications belong in the medical history).",
          "Periods when the patient believes help would have been useful but did not seek it."
        ] }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "sim", id: "sim-opposite", label: "Interview: Just the opposite" }]
    },

    {
      id: "bipolar-trap",
      title: "The bipolar trap: mania, hypomania, and the people who remember",
      lede: "Mr. Evans, part two: a diagnostic impression that collapses with one question, and what mania looks like up close.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "If the interview had stopped at melancholic major depression, it would have been wrong. Asked whether there had ever been a time when he felt the opposite, with days of little need for sleep and the sense of being ready to take on the world, Mr. Evans slowly recalled his first year as a police officer: days on a couple of hours of sleep, colleagues nicknaming him for his nonstop talk, projects started and never finished, a wish to help everyone that led him to try to release prisoners, and a hospitalization arranged by his chief. There was a second, briefer episode he had thought nothing of. He is best understood as having bipolar I disorder in a depressed phase (12-month prevalence about 0.6%, with a lifetime suicide risk at least fifteen times that of the general population)." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Why the trap springs", text: "As interviewer and patient become absorbed in the depressive material, no contextual cue ever suggests mania, so the question is never asked, and the depressed patient is too preoccupied to volunteer a history that, as we will see, is often sealed over anyway. The only protection is a rule: ask every patient about current and past manic symptoms." },
        { type: "table", head: ["", "Manic episode", "Hypomanic episode"], rows: [
          ["Mood and energy", "Abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive, or irritable mood plus persistently increased activity or energy", "Same quality of change"],
          ["Duration", "At least one week, most of the day nearly every day (any duration if hospitalized)", "At least four consecutive days"],
          ["Symptoms", "Three or more (four if mood is only irritable) of: grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, pressured talk, flight of ideas or racing thoughts, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity or agitation, risky pleasurable pursuits", "Same list and threshold"],
          ["Severity", "Marked impairment, or hospitalization to prevent harm, or any psychotic feature", "Unequivocal, observable change from baseline, but no marked impairment, no hospitalization, no psychosis (psychosis makes it mania by definition)"],
          ["Treatment-emergent", "An episode that emerges on an antidepressant or ECT but persists at full syndromal level beyond the treatment's physiological effect counts, and makes the diagnosis bipolar I", "Same rule, with a caution: one or two symptoms such as irritability or edginess after an antidepressant are not enough"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Bipolar I disorder requires at least one lifetime manic episode; hypomanic episodes are common in it but not required, and a course of manias only, with no depressions, is rare. Mean onset of the first episode is around 18, though adolescents and some children show manic symptoms, and a first mania after 50 should send you looking for structural brain disease: frontal tumors, or neurocognitive disorders that affect behavior, personality, and language before memory. Manic episodes come on over days, startle patient and family, last weeks to months, end more abruptly than depressions, and in about 60% of cases lead straight into a major depressive episode. Many patients have a signature way of switching, a fingerprint that helps everyone predict the next one. Ask patient and family about early warning signs that may build over weeks; Shea's example is a father whose sons knew that when a particular feathered hat came out of the closet, he would be manic within two weeks. Such idiosyncratic harbingers are gold for relapse prevention." },
        { type: "p", text: "Classic [[Euphoric mania]] is nearly the mirror image of withdrawn depression: elation, a grandiose sense of mission, enormous energy, a few hours of sleep or none, often hypersexuality. Speech is fast, loud, pressured, full of jokes and barely related topics. [[Flight of ideas]] is speeded speech and thought in which logical links are usually preserved but tenuous, with abrupt shifts triggered by external stimuli, puns, or humor; at the extreme it becomes incoherent. Shea's illustration is a man who accosted him outside a library study room, announced a world record in jumping jacks, stood far too close and followed when Shea stepped back, held intense unbroken eye contact, performed push-ups and a moonwalk unasked, and then, hooking a passing student, slid by wordplay from his own fame to a famous architect's house. Beyond the criteria, euphoric mania disrupts the nonverbal rules of conversation: invaded personal space, loud voice, and unyielding gaze together create an uncomfortable surge of [[Immediacy]], sometimes compounded by body odor because bathing has fallen off the to-do list. Patients assume a false familiarity with strangers and show what Shea calls a demonstration propensity. Spotting these same tendencies in muted form is how early hypomania is caught, and early intervention can cut the ferocity of an episode and the doses needed to end it." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "All is not rosy inside a euphoric mania", text: "Irritability lies just under the surface, affective lability swings the patient from laughter to tears in moments, and impulsivity supplies the dark side: substance use, car wrecks, violence, suicide. Limit setting or an attempt to structure the interview can flip the mood instantly; when Shea suggested moving to the lobby, the jovial man snapped a warning at him, then smiled and went on talking. On the unit or in the emergency department, be especially alert with a 'happy' manic patient who is pacing and intent on getting his way. Such patients can turn hostile, even violent, within seconds." },
        { type: "p", text: "About 60% of patients with bipolar disorder show psychotic symptoms at some point. Most manic delusions are [[Mood-congruent / mood-incongruent|mood-congruent]]: religious destiny, special powers, identity as an extraordinary figure. Mood-incongruent content (control by others, bizarre or paranoid themes) occurs in roughly 30 to 40% of manic patients. About a quarter of patients with bipolar disorder become psychotic during depressions, more than four times the rate in unipolar depression, and clinicians often miss it. Hallucinations are common in mania, and command hallucinations have driven rare but catastrophic self-mutilation and homicide; the psychosis chapters teach how to uncover such ideation." },
        { type: "p", text: "A telephone call to Mr. Evans's elderly mother filled in what he had not said: the episode was more than thirty years ago, in a small rural force; over days he began talking constantly, declared himself a one-man comedy act, announced a three-volume book on police kindness, went unwashed for a week, called sleep something for lower life forms, and had trouble concentrating and remembering. She also mentioned, almost in passing, that he had pulled a knife on his father during the break, a fact neither he nor his fiancée had disclosed. Three lessons. People naturally downplay past mania; the behaviors are embarrassing or guilt-laden, and rationalization, repression, and denial bury the details, so past episodes are commonly [[Sealing over|sealed over]]. Whenever an initial assessment turns up a hint of manic or hypomanic history, interview family and other collateral sources for the true severity. And neither patients nor families spontaneously mention hypomanic episodes, which seem trivial beside the manias and depressions, unless asked directly." },
        { type: "p", text: "The mother's remark about his thinking also points to a real phenomenon: mania, like depression, can produce specific cognitive deficits that are not just the by-product of being wound up, and some persist into euthymia. Weeks of sleep loss, dehydration, and exhaustion can tip a manic patient into [[Delirium]]. Very rarely, confusion is part of the manic presentation itself; Shea once saw a rapidly cycling student who seemed briefly bewildered (unsure how to drive home) an hour before each switch, a picture that demands ruling out partial complex seizures." },
        { type: "p", text: "Although the DSM-5 has no multiaxial system, Shea still records general psychiatric disorders, personality disorders, and medical disorders as separate headings, because it forces him to look for the last two in every patient and tells the next reader of the record that he looked. 'None found' under personality disorders is information; a blank is not. Mr. Evans's formulation: bipolar I disorder, current episode depressed with melancholic features; alcohol use disorder in remission; no personality disorder found; chronic osteoarthritis of the knees." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c9p1", label: "Practice: screening for past mania" }, { kind: "sim", id: "sim-opposite", label: "Interview: Just the opposite" }]
    },

    {
      id: "mixed-states",
      title: "Mixed states and dysphoric mania",
      lede: "Danny Ramirez: a depression so palpable that it hides the mania inside it.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Danny Ramirez", text: "Summarized from the chapter. An 18-year-old high school senior, a second-generation Latino whose father is a school principal, an excellent student and community volunteer whose environmental project was noted in his state's legislative record. Severe OCD began at 8, followed by years of what were assumed to be reactive depressive symptoms. In high school the depression deepened: frequent crying, poor concentration, trouble falling asleep, mildly reduced appetite, a mood he describes as constant and life as worthless, striking irritability and bitter anger at the world, a darkening personality, three episodes of superficial wrist cutting, and hostile, dramatic scenes with parents he is otherwise close to. His therapist, frightened for his safety, has requested a consultation hoping an antidepressant will be started." },
        { type: "p", text: "The DSM-IV-TR defined a mixed episode as full criteria for mania and for depression at the same time for a week. Shea has met exactly one such patient in his career, a technician who could barely stay in his chair, spoke in a rapid pressured tangential stream whose content was pure guilt and suicidal rumination, and switched from tears to laughter within seconds. That picture was real but it was neither prototypic nor common, and the narrow definition left most mixed patients unrecognized. Partial mixed states, with depressive and manic symptoms in varying numbers and combinations, are common; Goodwin and Jamison estimate that with broader criteria more than half of bipolar episodes would count as mixed. Mania and depression may be better understood as a continuum than as opposite poles." },
        { type: "p", text: "This is rediscovery, not discovery. Kraepelin devoted a chapter to mixed states, described 'anxious mania' and 'excited depression,' and in 1899 regarded mixed presentations as the most common form of the illness, delineating six. Bleuler saw them as distinct, stable, and especially destructive, a judgment modern research has confirmed. Griesinger, in 1882, described a 'melancholia with destructive tendencies' that reads today like a dysphoric mania. The clinical stakes are high: patients who are deeply depressed with several manic symptoms, short of full mania, were for decades diagnosed with agitated depression, denied mood stabilizers, and sometimes made worse by antidepressants alone." },
        { type: "p", text: "The DSM-5 answer is dimensional. Once a patient meets full criteria for a manic or hypomanic episode, three depressive symptoms present most days (depressed mood or dysphoria, anhedonia, observable psychomotor retardation, fatigue, worthlessness or excessive guilt, recurrent thoughts of death or suicidality) earn the specifier [[Mixed features|with mixed features]]. Once a patient meets full criteria for a major depressive episode, three manic symptoms most days (elevated or expansive mood, grandiosity, pressured talk, flight of ideas or racing thoughts, increased energy or goal-directed activity, risky pursuits, decreased need for sleep as distinct from insomnia) earn the same specifier. If both episodes are met at once, the diagnosis is manic episode with mixed features, because full mania is the more severe condition. Mixed features in a depressive episode are a recognized risk factor for later bipolar I or II disorder, and the anxious distress specifier can be stacked on top. There are as many mixed states as there are combinations of depressive, manic, and anxious symptoms." },
        { type: "p", text: "[[Dysphoric mania]] is Shea's candidate for a mixed subtype that will eventually earn its own specifier, the way melancholia did. He is careful to agree with Strakowski that new categories need validation first, but he argues the phenomenology is worth hunting for now because it changes treatment. The nearest look-alike in a seriously depressed patient is an [[Agitated depression]], which also features racing thoughts, pacing, and irritability. Age helps: agitated depressions cluster in the elderly and are uncommon in adolescents and young adults. The feel of the depression helps more." },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Agitated depression", items: [
            "Overlay of anxiety and fretting about finances, health, business, or frankly delusional disease and ruin.",
            "The patient feels overwhelmed and helpless, lost in agitated disorganization, almost paralyzed by fear.",
            "Attention turns inward: inadequacy, guilt, somatic worry, withdrawal from a harsh world.",
            "Asked for ideas about solving the problem, the patient is befuddled or irritated by the question."
          ] },
          b: { title: "Dysphoric mania", items: [
            "High anxiety too, but the patient does not look or feel helpless; the dominant sensation is of being driven to do something, anything, now.",
            "The drive can feel foreign, as if something inside is pushing; it colors suicidal ideation ('I don't want to die, but something is pushing me to').",
            "Attention turns outward: specific people, the culture, the wrongs of the world, with a bitter animosity Griesinger captured well, and sometimes violent imagery.",
            "Often has quite specific plans of action, and turns angry fast when thwarted, even over something as small as a cigarette."
          ] }
        },
        { type: "p", text: "Dysphoric manias are missed in Danny's age group for predictable reasons: unfamiliarity with mixed states (reinforced by the old narrow definition), the sheer intensity of the stated depression ('I'm depressed all the time, I hate life') that crowds out every other hypothesis, and a symptom profile in which the classic euphoric features are absent and the manic features that are present are dysphoric. Danny's thoughts raced and pressed unpleasantly (patients with dysphoric mania want to escape their racing thoughts; patients in euphoric mania enjoy them and call their thinking clearer than ever), but his speech was only occasionally and mildly pressured. He did not pace, had no flight of ideas, distractibility, grandiosity, or spending sprees, only mild hypersexuality, and was pessimistic about his future. He stayed up somewhat late, once doing pull-ups on a tree limb at midnight, without the progressive sleep loss of classic mania, and never showed a moment of euphoria. The camouflage is nearly complete." },
        { type: "steps", items: [
          "Persistent anger as a leading symptom. Danny's was broad: betrayal by friends, contempt for current events, stormy intense friendships, dismissive hostility toward parents he loved. Ask who the patient is angry at and how much of each day is spent irritated; ask whether the world is fair and be ready for a diatribe. The anger of a dysphoric mania is a persistent mood state, not the intermittent rage after interpersonal slights seen in borderline personality disorder, though reactivity may sit inside it. It can also become violent, planned or impulsive.",
          "An angst of unusual intensity with a bitter tone. The therapist's fear was itself a sign. Coupled with manic impulsivity, this angst raises suicide risk substantially.",
          "Discordance between the angst and the neurovegetative symptoms. A patient this tortured should have severe sleep, appetite, energy, and concentration disturbance; Danny's were mild (some trouble falling asleep, no early morning awakening, normal energy and concentration). Shea suspects manic drive overrides the classic neurovegetative picture. When the pain is out of proportion to the physiology, suspect that the patient is not simply depressed."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "With such a patient, explore fantasies and online habits: which websites he frequents (watch for anger at the culture, violence, suicide), how often violent images come, whether he pictures himself doing something violent. If psychosis appears, intensify the search for dangerous ideation, including command hallucinations and psychotic self-mutilation. Danny, at this point, had dark musings but no violent ideation and no psychosis. Two historical tip-offs raise suspicion of any mixed state in a depressed patient: a family history loaded with bipolar I and II disorder, and often with depression, anxiety, and alcohol and substance disorders (Danny's was full of depression, alcohol dependence, and anxiety, with one grandfather adopted and his line unknown); and a personal history of earlier bipolar processes such as bipolar II disorder or substance- or medication-induced episodes. Bipolar depressions in general tend toward atypical features (mood reactivity, overeating, oversleeping, heavy fatigue), more mixed presentations in the young, more psychotic features, and a significantly higher suicide rate. Patients in dysphoric manias often respond well to mood stabilizers, poorly to antidepressants alone, and sometimes catastrophically to antidepressants that unleash the underlying rage." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "sim", id: "sim-angst", label: "Interview: Before the antidepressant" }, { kind: "drill", id: "mood-pictures", label: "Drill: Mood presentations" }]
    },

    {
      id: "soft-bipolarity",
      title: "Before any antidepressant: bipolar II, induced episodes, cyclothymia",
      lede: "The soft end of the spectrum, the opposite error of over-diagnosis, and the suicidal ideation that a medication can unleash.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "First, the flip side. Missing bipolar process is serious; so is inventing it. Children, adolescents, and young adults with angry outbursts and disruptive behavior at home or school are regularly labeled bipolar and placed on mood stabilizers and antipsychotics they do not need. Many are reacting to bullying or violence at home; many have ADHD or oppositional defiant disorder; some have a [[Depressive equivalent]], depression expressed through anger and outbursts rather than low mood and neurovegetative signs, perhaps because of the maturing brain. The DSM-5 created [[DMDD|disruptive mood dysregulation disorder]] precisely to capture this group. It cannot be diagnosed if manic or hypomanic criteria have ever been met or if the outbursts occur only within a major depressive episode, and the term bipolar disorder is reserved for episodic presentations." },
        { type: "p", text: "[[Bipolar II disorder]] is one or more major depressive episodes with at least one [[Hypomanic episode]] and never a full mania. It is common (lifetime prevalence around 1 to 2%), and an estimated 25 to 50% of patients with recurrent major depression have hypomanic features. Hypomania differs from mania not in the kind of symptoms but in severity and duration: no marked impairment, no hospitalization, no psychosis, and four days rather than a week. Some patients enjoy mild hypomania and are briefly more productive and witty, but the episodes are usually more disruptive than useful: feeling scattered, 'not myself,' impulsive drinking or regretted liaisons, and a slide into depression that can be severe. A significant share progress to bipolar I. Smith argued persuasively that the four-day rule is too rigid, since many patients have one- or two-day bursts, and Shea has seen several in a single day, which the ICD-10's description of rapid cycling acknowledges. Clinically, he hunts for briefer bursts than the DSM-5 requires, and for dysphoric hypomanic bursts as well as euphoric ones: dark and violent imagery, agitation, trouble falling asleep, irritability and anger, unpleasant racing thoughts, destructive impulsivity, a sense of psychological darkness." },
        { type: "p", text: "Two screening questions, asked after the depression has been explored. The first describes euphoric hypomania (spells of hours, days, or weeks of feeling unusually happy, super-energized, ready to take on the world) and ends with a clause that does real work: and you cannot explain why, and it feels almost odd to you. Many people with true hypomania are puzzled by their own mood shifts, especially when the shift follows depression with no good news to account for it; adding puzzlement to the question screens out ordinary great days. The second asks about the dysphoric version: thoughts suddenly racing in an unpleasant, uncontrollable rush, an angry tone, life turning darker, again unexplained and odd. Finding bipolar II disorder can transform a life: a mood stabilizer may improve it markedly, might (unproven) prevent evolution to bipolar I, and antidepressants alone are generally considered contraindicated because they can unleash further hypomania or a full mania." },
        { type: "p", text: "[[Substance/medication-induced bipolar disorder]] (historically 'bipolar III') is the patient whose manic or hypomanic symptoms appear only with a triggering agent: antidepressants most famously, but also amantadine, isotretinoin, varenicline, steroids, stimulants, PCP, bath salts, St. John's wort, light therapy, and ECT. Most patients return to baseline soon after the agent is removed; if symptoms persist a month beyond discontinuation, the diagnosis becomes bipolar I or II. Over years, a minority go on to bipolar II and some to bipolar I, and it is unknown whether the agent accelerated an illness that was coming anyway or triggered one that otherwise would not have appeared; either way a genetic predisposition is likely. Hence Shea's rule: before starting any antidepressant, search aggressively for current hypomanic symptoms, past hypomanic or manic episodes, and a family history of bipolar process. Where history suggests hypomania, some clinicians try psychotherapy alone first and, if an antidepressant is later needed, load a mood stabilizer beforehand. Even lamotrigine has been reported to trigger suicidal ideation. The treatment debates lie beyond an interviewing text; the interviewing rule does not." },
        { type: "p", text: "Danny closes the loop. Asked how antidepressants had gone over the years, his parents looked at each other and groaned. At 8, an antidepressant for his OCD brought a 90% remission within three weeks and, within five or six, agitation, insomnia, and 'an attitude.' Two months in, this conscientious, well-behaved child was oppositional, screaming and swearing at his parents, and once pulled a kitchen knife on the family dog he loved. Off the drug, the behavior vanished in two weeks and the OCD returned in force. Over the next two years every low-dose antidepressant trial produced dysphoric manic symptoms, one with a brief paranoid psychosis. He is a textbook substance/medication-induced bipolar history, and OCD and other serious anxiety disorders appear to be even more common in bipolar than in unipolar patients." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Suicidal ideation unleashed by a medication", text: "Whether you prescribed the drug, a colleague did, or you are the consultant, suspect that new suicidal ideation is an unleashing phenomenon rather than a deepening depression when it is unexpected and unlike the patient's past ideation, when the patient says it feels different, and when it arrives with a peculiar intensity and impulsiveness alongside other dysphoric manic phenomena: agitation (pacing, or a surge of finger picking, hair twirling, foot twitching), angry outbursts, unpleasant racing thoughts, marked trouble falling asleep, intense angst. Shea's example is a woman in her late twenties whose antidepressant he had just increased: within ten days, with no new stressor, she was screaming at her family, tearful and labile, feeling like a different person, and frightened by a sudden compelling urge to throw a hair dryer into her bath, though she insisted she did not want to die. Her last suicidal thought had been mild and eight years earlier. Two days after a large dose reduction she was almost herself; by day five the ideation was gone and never returned." },
        { type: "p", text: "Two last diagnoses Danny calls to mind. [[Cyclothymic disorder]]: over two years in an adult, frequent periods of hypomanic symptoms and of depressive symptoms, neither reaching full episode criteria, present at least half the time with no symptom-free stretch longer than two months. Less disruptive than bipolar disorder, but painful and puzzling, with a 15 to 50% chance of evolving into bipolar I or II; a mood stabilizer and psychotherapy can change a life. [[Rapid cycling]] is a specifier for bipolar I or II: four or more mood episodes of any kind in twelve months, each separated by full remission or by an immediate switch to the opposite pole. It characterizes roughly 10 to 20% of bipolar patients, is more common in women although bipolar disorder overall is evenly distributed, and carries a poorer prognosis and more complex medication regimens." },
        { type: "p", text: "The consultant judged that Danny had been misdiagnosed with major depression, that his picture was a mixed bipolar state with dysphoric features, that antidepressants were contraindicated unless preceded by a sound mood-stabilizer trial, and that lithium should be started, with the expectation that the anger and acting out (easily misread as emerging personality pathology) would clear while some true depressive symptoms remained for psychotherapy, which would then work better: a healing matrix effect from Chapter 7. He warned that without a mood stabilizer Danny risked a severe psychotic mania. A brief lithium trial worked, but Danny rejected the diagnosis, later psychiatrists called it a personality disorder, and lithium lapsed. In his third year of college he had a ferocious psychotic mania with command hallucinations to blind himself and demonic delusions that took five months to bring into remission. With excellent care afterward and his own skill with his medications, he was symptom-free four years later, graduated, employed, and in a steady relationship. A well-trained clinician in a single fifty-minute interview had been more accurate than clinicians who saw him for years." },
        { type: "p", text: "Formulating Danny under the DSM-5 is awkward, and Shea says so: the mixed-features specifier still requires a full depressive or manic episode, which Danny met neither of, and the criteria lean toward euphoric symptoms. The honest label at the time was other specified bipolar and related disorder (dysphoric mania), with OCD with good insight, a rule-out of bipolar disorder due to another medical condition, no personality disorder, and a recommended work-up (CT, EEG) for tumors or epilepsy. After college the diagnosis became bipolar I disorder, mixed, with mood-incongruent psychotic features. The phrase 'bipolar I disorder, mixed, dysphoric mania' captures his inner world with descriptive essence in a minimum of words." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Mixed presentations are more common in late adolescents and young adults and are easily misdiagnosed as irritable or agitated major depression, or as borderline or histrionic personality disorder. When you see those behavioral pictures, interview carefully for dysphoric mixed states; and watch for the reverse error, since some patients have both.",
          "Monitor the potential for self-cutting, suicide, and violence toward others at the first interview and in ongoing sessions. Dysphoric manias may also predispose to psychosis.",
          "Listen to family members on symptoms, medication responses, and diagnostic impressions. They are often right."
        ] }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "sim", id: "sim-angst", label: "Interview: Before the antidepressant" }, { kind: "practice", id: "c9p1", label: "Practice: screening for past mania" }]
    },

    {
      id: "disguise",
      title: "Depression in disguise: denial, culture, atypical features, and the organic screen",
      lede: "Mr. Whitstone: a man who says he is not depressed, and a consultant who keeps thinking organic.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Mr. Whitstone", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A distinguished 62-year-old businessman admitted to a general hospital for what his family calls paranoid behavior, now refusing all care including intravenous lines and medication. He grills the emergency consultant about his training and purpose, then cooperates, tense, complaining that his thinking and concentration have failed and that no one understands. He denies feeling particularly depressed, admits poor appetite and sleep, and is most preoccupied with a belief that someone in his company means to kill him. He has not returned to work since bypass surgery six months earlier. He is alert and fully oriented, with three family members at the bedside." },
        { type: "p", text: "Since the surgery he had shown trouble falling asleep, broken sleep, loss of appetite and weight, poor concentration, and anhedonia, and his wife and children described him as withdrawn, sad, and not himself. Yet he denied depression, and about 30% of people who meet criteria for major depression do (Klein's estimate). Shea's approach is a ladder. Begin open: how would you describe your mood these past weeks, tell me how you have been feeling, would you say you have felt depressed? If depressed is refused, change the word: sad, unhappy. Many patients deny depression and admit sadness. The question of when the patient last felt like crying is a [[Gentle assumption|gentle assumption]] from Chapter 5 that treats crying as common and acceptable and asks only about the urge, so a self-conscious man can answer without admitting tears; 'have you been crying?' offers only denial or confession. If depression and sadness are both vigorously denied, as here, ask whether he has been feeling himself lately, or up to par." },
        { type: "p", text: "Culture can hide a depression or relabel it. Among Latino/a patients depression is often framed as trouble with one's nerves, and asking whether stress brings on [[Nervios]] opens the door; nervios also runs to somatic complaints such as headaches ('brain aches'), trembling, tingling, and dizziness, and as a syndrome can span depression, anxiety, dissociation, and even psychosis. An ataque de nervios may meet criteria for a panic attack, and in Puerto Rican and Dominican patients it can include sudden trembling, uncontrollable crying or screaming, aggressive or suicidal behavior, and depersonalization, so that a clinician unfamiliar with the pattern mislabels an ordinary panic attack as histrionic or dangerous. Stereotyped groupings hide real differences between Latino/a, Hispanic, Dominican, Cuban, and other heritages. Elsewhere the somatic metaphor shapes the label itself: problems of the heart in Middle Eastern cultures, being heartbroken among the Hopi, being weak, tired, or imbalanced in Chinese and other Asian cultures. Stigma also varies, and with it how readily symptoms are shared. When the interview is inadequate, a shrewd clinician knows it; Mr. Whitstone's family had watched him be pervasively depressed for two months." },
        { type: "p", text: "Asked how he had not felt himself, Mr. Whitstone spoke with real distress about a memo he had always been able to dictate in twenty minutes that took him two days and a hung-up phone. Cognitive complaints deserve their own questions: has thinking sped up (hypomania, mania, mixed states, agitated depression) or slowed down (melancholic or withdrawn depression); are decisions harder or more frustrating; do thoughts seem disconnected or confused; is it hard to hold a train of thought, to read, or to follow conversation. The cognitive examination later in the book sorts dementias masquerading as depression from depressions masquerading as dementia (pseudodementia)." },
        { type: "p", text: "Not every depression looks classic, and the DSM-5 [[Atypical features|atypical features]] specifier is poorly named: 15.7 to 36.6% of depressions qualify, up to 50% in bipolar II depression and dysthymia, and atypical pictures are more common in bipolar I, especially mixed presentations. Spotting them matters because they prompt a thorough hunt for bipolar process (and caution with antidepressants, or psychotherapy alone, if it is found), because even without hypomania they warrant vigilance for an unleashed mania once an antidepressant starts, and because purely atypical depressions unresponsive to SSRIs or tricyclics may respond preferentially to monoamine oxidase inhibitors and cognitive therapy. The criteria: a major depressive episode with [[Mood reactivity]], plus two of four secondary symptoms." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "[[Mood reactivity]]: unlike classic depression, which persists regardless of environment, the patient can brighten by at least half or become transiently euthymic in response to a good event (a favorite film, a compliment, a weekend away with a new partner), sometimes for hours or longer while the reinforcer lasts. Some authors doubt it should be mandatory.",
          "[[Rejection sensitivity]]: a lifelong trait present during and between episodes (the rare DSM-5 instance of a personality trait inside a non-personality diagnosis, which is why histrionic, borderline, and narcissistic disorders co-occur). Innocuous remarks, texts, or posts are taken the wrong way, producing hurt, embarrassment, and anger, a trail of lost jobs and friendships and stormy romances, and sometimes drug and alcohol use for solace. In one series it was the most common secondary symptom (71%), followed by overeating and leaden paralysis (47% each) and oversleeping (35%). Not all atypical patients have it.",
          "Increased appetite or significant weight gain during the episode, the reverse of the classic picture.",
          "Hypersomnia: ten hours a day including naps, or two hours more than when well, often with a need to sleep in the daytime and a sudden perkiness if something pleasant turns up.",
          "[[Leaden paralysis]]: a poorly named, unpleasant heaviness in the arms or, more often, legs, described as weak, rubbery, or 'just not right,' sometimes frightening in a medical way. In older patients make sure it is not peripheral arterial disease."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Both classic and atypical depressions, especially in primary care, may present as pain or other somatic complaints; vigorous pursuit of the neurovegetative and atypical symptoms will often find the depression underneath. The setting matters: in a given year about 10 to 12% of United States adults see a primary care clinician while suffering a major psychiatric disorder, more than half with two or more; roughly half of people who die by suicide have seen a primary care clinician within a month, and one in five within a week. The suicide assessment skills of Chapter 17 may be among the main hopes of lowering the suicide rate." },
        { type: "p", text: "Psychosis also occurs in unipolar depression, especially agitated depressions and in the elderly. [[Mood-congruent / mood-incongruent|Mood-congruent]] depressive themes are inadequacy, guilt, disease, poverty, nihilism, and deserved punishment; mood-incongruent content is more bizarre or peculiar: paranoia, possession, thought insertion, control. Mr. Whitstone's conviction that a colleague wants him dead would count as mood-incongruent psychotic features." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Think organic", text: "Shea saved his most important point for last. Mr. Whitstone had features of [[Delirium]] in the nursing notes (auditory hallucinations, rapid shifts of affect, fluctuating consciousness), had not been himself since cardiac surgery, was anticoagulated (emboli from the heart, or hemorrhage), and was significantly dehydrated. Ask about prescription and over-the-counter drugs: cimetidine, propranolol, methyldopa, reserpine, amantadine, steroids, oral contraceptives, opiates, even thiazides through electrolyte shifts, and prescribed synthetic opioids such as oxycodone, now a leading cause of overdose deaths. Think of hypothyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, lupus, hepatitis, and carcinoma (pancreatic cancer is notorious for presenting as depression). Anderson's six categories: drugs and poisons, metabolic and endocrine disturbances, infections, degenerative diseases such as multiple sclerosis, neoplasms, and miscellaneous conditions. You will sometimes miss an organic cause despite looking; there is no excuse for not having looked." },
        { type: "p", text: "The moment Shea finds it easiest to forget organic causes is when the patient arrives with an obvious life stressor: unemployment, housing, divorce, a death. Ample reason to be depressed does not exclude a concurrent physical cause, and separation events often precede medical illness. Think holistically in both directions: apparent adjustment reactions can hide something biologically ominous, and an obvious endogenous depression may be sustained by a family dynamic no one has mentioned. His formulation for Mr. Whitstone: psychotic disorder due to another medical condition, condition unknown, provisional; rule out delirium; rule out major depressive disorder with mood-incongruent psychotic features; possible paranoid or compulsive traits per the family; significant dehydration; status post bypass; rule out embolism or hemorrhage. The full work-up (chemistries, EEG, head CT, lumbar puncture, echocardiogram) was normal, and he stayed symptomatic after rehydration, so a major depression with mood-incongruent psychotic features, perhaps with brief episodes of mild delirium from dehydration, became the most likely answer." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c9p4", label: "Practice: when depression is denied" }]
    },

    {
      id: "persistence",
      title: "Persistence, the history of the present illness, and the red herrings",
      lede: "Ms. Wilkins: 'depressed for years' and the questions that showed otherwise.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Ms. Wilkins", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A 26-year-old woman in a faded, wrinkled dress enters hesitantly, says she feels horrible and very depressed, tearfully mentions thinking the night before about killing herself, and lists sleep disturbance, low energy, low libido, and increased appetite. She says she has been depressed for years and looks it; the interviewer feels a growing wish to help and a growing concern. There is anger too, aimed at a best friend who has betrayed her trust. She denies current suicidal or homicidal ideation, says she feels in control now, and wants medication and therapy." },
        { type: "p", text: "A few questions changed the picture. Asked whether the depression of 'years' ever lifted, she allowed that she had good days, including a couple the previous week right before the fight with her friend; on the Friday she had been having a great day until the friend ruined it. Asked whether she had ever had a full two weeks of unbroken low mood, she reached back to age 19, when she was depressed for about four months. She is probably not in a sustained depressive episode now. Her history fits [[Persistent depressive disorder]], with fluctuating neurovegetative symptoms, and a major depression at 19 in remission that had responded to paroxetine." },
        { type: "p", text: "Two principles follow. A careful history of the presenting disorder, with close attention to time course and duration, is the foundation of the diagnostic interview. And persistence must be rigorously tested, because people whose depressed feelings come and go describe them as unrelenting unless questioned carefully; misery overshadows the normal hours. The criteria require each symptom nearly every day for at least two weeks. Significant fluctuation sends you elsewhere. Shea's observation: 'I've been depressed for years' is, oddly, often a sign that classic major depression is absent, and that the symptoms instead track environmental rewards, as in some personality disorders, dysthymia, substance use, and some atypical depressions. Ask whether mood shifts within a single day, whether the patient would call herself moody, and whether a friend or something to do can perk her up quickly; on a yes, ask for [[Behavioral incident|behavioral incidents]]. The roller-coaster continuum question (symptoms that stay day after day versus symptoms that come and go) is a reliable discriminator." },
        { type: "p", text: "Ms. Wilkins is the mirror image of Danny: a personality disorder misread as a major depression. Missing a borderline personality disorder costs the patient effective therapies such as dialectical behavior therapy, risks triage to inexperienced clinicians, invites dangerous dependency on the therapist, exposes her to needless medications, and distracts from suicide risk. Further interviewing revealed years of angry outbursts (a hammer through a window), severe loneliness, boredom and emptiness, confusion about sexual relationships, a series of overdoses, and long-standing wrist cutting and fingertip burning that erupted abruptly after perceived interpersonal stress even in the middle of good days. No family history of bipolar process, no manic unleashing on antidepressants, and a history of childhood sexual abuse, which the personality disorder chapter shows is not uncommon in this group. She met criteria for borderline personality disorder. The principle: however depressed someone looks, the diagnosis is not always major depression, and people with borderline personality disorder have a particular knack for looking severely upset." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) and cyclothymic disorder.",
          "Borderline personality disorder, and histrionic, narcissistic, avoidant, dependent, or obsessive-compulsive personality disorders.",
          "Alcohol or drug use.",
          "Adjustment disorder with depressed mood.",
          "Medical causes such as hypothyroidism.",
          "[[V-codes]]: marital, housing, or economic problems producing normal depressive responses to situational stress."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "These common mimics share one feature: mood and symptoms fluctuate significantly, unlike a major depressive episode. Mislabeling them leads to triage and prescribing errors, and for Ms. Wilkins, whose record was a trail of empty bottles from overdoses, a premature antidepressant could be fatal." },
        { type: "p", text: "Taking the history of the presenting disorder is harder than it sounds: memory gaps, unconscious distortion, conscious or histrionic distortion, and misunderstood questions all intrude, and they compound when the clinician has no plan and gets lost in facts. Shea divides the history into an early phase, a mid-phase, and a recent phase (the two months before the interview) and, given time pressure, emphasizes the early and recent phases. The early phase shows the pathology unfolding naturally and before medications blurred it; in a patient with hallucinations and depression, depression first then psychosis suggests a mood disorder, while psychosis and agitation first, with depression later, suggests schizophrenia (Chapter 11), information that stays buried unless you or a family member is asked. The recent phase establishes current functioning and current diagnosis, with one trap: a partially treated patient may look sub-threshold, so ask about the picture just before the medication began." },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Approach one: start early", items: [
            "Steer the patient quickly to the beginning and move chronologically, lightly over the middle.",
            "Stressors and responses emerge naturally; the result is detailed and well ordered.",
            "Risk: histories are complex and fascinating, and the clock runs out before the current picture is clear."
          ] },
          b: { title: "Approach two: start recent", items: [
            "Nail down current symptoms, stressors, and functioning first, then jump to the early phase and come forward.",
            "Patients often prefer talking about now; an early differential forms and guides the rest of the questioning.",
            "Frame the window explicitly ('just the last two weeks') and remind the patient of it repeatedly, because pain pulls people back to earlier symptoms without announcing the shift."
          ] }
        },
        { type: "exchange", title: "Dating the onset", lines: [
          { who: "Clin.", t: "When did this low period first start for you?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Right after the fiesta in May. Everything fell apart after that." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Think back to the months before the fiesta. Were you completely yourself then, or already a bit off?", tag: "second ask, with an anchor" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Actually, I'd been low since my son left for Manila in February. I just didn't call it anything." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "What did you notice first?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Tired all the time. Teary. I'm not sure when the mornings got so hard, though. Maybe later. I can't pin it down." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "That's hard to pin down, and you're doing well. Let's stay with the fiesta week. Was getting out of bed hard then?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Yes. I didn't even want to cook for the guests." }
        ], note: "Composed. Patients first give the date things got bad, which is weeks or months after onset, so ask a second time and push earlier. Carlat's time-related [[Anchor question|anchor question]] (a holiday, a family event) primes recall, and when memory frays, return to the anchor and ask about concrete symptoms there." },
        { type: "p", text: "Three more rule-outs from her case. Peripartum: mood disturbance in the months after birth, far more severe than ordinary postpartum blues, sometimes preceded by mood symptoms during the pregnancy, able to evolve into bipolar and mixed states with psychosis (Chapter 11), in which you must ask about danger to the infant (a belief the child is possessed, command hallucinations to harm it); the DSM-5 specifier is with peripartum onset whether symptoms began before or after delivery. Grief: a full major depressive episode can occur during acute bereavement and, if present or if the symptoms are severe and atypical for the person's culture, is diagnosed alongside it; bereavement that lasts too long or intensifies over months becomes major depression. Phenomenology helps: normal grief comes in waves, pangs triggered by reminders, with milder and less persistent neurovegetative symptoms and guilt that is specific to the deceased (not visiting enough, not saying goodbye), whereas depressive guilt generalizes to worthlessness and inadequacy. Uncomplicated bereavement is one of nearly a hundred [[V-codes]], which are not mental disorders but flags for aspects of the matrix (marital problems, partner abuse, work, finances, housing, deployment) where intervention may matter. Adjustment disorder with depressed mood requires a clear stressor within three months and a response out of proportion or impairing, and it is dropped the moment full criteria for a major depressive episode are met." },
        { type: "p", text: "Her formulation: persistent depressive disorder; a V-code for personal history of childhood sexual abuse so that treatment attends to it; rule out recurrent major depressive disorder, with and without atypical features (she had mood reactivity, rejection sensitivity, and increased appetite, a pattern seen with some regularity in borderline and other personality disorders); borderline personality disorder as the principal diagnosis, a label worth using when the personality disorder is the main problem or the reason for admission; no medical disorders." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c9p5", label: "Practice: testing persistence" }]
    },

    {
      id: "family-history",
      title: "Dysthymia and the family history",
      lede: "Mr. Collier: a man who reminds himself of his father, and how to ask the question that remark invites.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Mr. Collier", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A 26-year-old man and his wife come to the psychiatric emergency room. He is neatly dressed, strong-jawed, quick and authoritative in speech, and the interviewer can picture him interrogating his wife. He complains bitterly of depression since adolescence, says he reminds himself of his father, sleeps in the daytime and tires easily, yet has good energy for things he likes, a robust appetite and sex drive, and real enjoyment of friends and football. He feels intermittently worthless and lazy, is upset at being sharp with his children, and the day before slapped his five-year-old daughter's face, which frightened him into coming. Fleeting suicidal thoughts have taken the form of stepping in front of a vehicle so that his wife would collect the insurance; he denies current ideation and says that helping him will help his family." },
        { type: "p", text: "Like Ms. Wilkins, he fluctuates, has had no two-week stretch of pervasive low mood, and so does not meet criteria for a major depressive episode; unlike her, he shows no borderline features, only mildly narcissistic traits that his wife confirms, and no manic or hypomanic symptoms beyond chronic irritability. Intermittent depression for more than ten years without a good stretch longer than a month or two is dysthymia, [[Persistent depressive disorder]], which responds to both antidepressants and psychotherapy. Its low-grade symptoms often lift quickly when something fun appears, a reactivity akin to that of atypical depression, and if full major depression ever supervenes it is added as a concurrent diagnosis; a major depression that has not fully remitted in two years also earns the added label." },
        { type: "p", text: "'I remind myself of my father' is an invitation. Genetic loading is real across the spectrum: twin studies put the heritability of bipolar disorder around 60 to 80%, relatives of patients with major depression carry more depression and depressive personalities, and the COGA study found that alcoholism and depression run together in families, partly for genetic reasons. A family history helps the patient in concrete ways. A relative's bipolar disorder sends you back to hunt for symptoms you may have passed over; psychosis or suicide in the family may tip a borderline decision toward closer follow-up or admission; a relative's good response to a specific medication predicts the patient's; and bipolar process in the family, or a relative's manic switch on an antidepressant, is one more reason for caution before prescribing one." },
        { type: "p", text: "Obstacles to a valid family history in fifty minutes: the patient does not know it, cannot concentrate well enough to report it accurately, protects the family, comes from a culture that forbids discussing it, or is asked badly. Only the last is yours to fix. The blanket question about mental illness in the family yields blanket negatives, because the patient may not realize you mean aunts, uncles, and cousins, or that alcoholism counts. Explain why you are asking (clues from relatives can suggest how to help), which also focuses the patient and encourages the memory search, and lead in from something already on the table." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "A lead-in and a widening", lines: [
          { who: "Clin.", t: "You said earlier that you remind yourself of your father. In what ways?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "He was always on edge. Shouting at all of us kids, even the baby. Troubled, I guess." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Do you think he was depressed?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Yeah. He'd never have seen anyone about it, but yeah." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "While we're on your father, I'd like to ask about the rest of the family. Sometimes problems in relatives give us clues about what will help you. These next questions are about blood relatives on both sides: parents, grandparents, aunts, uncles, cousins. Has anyone on your father's side had depression, or an illness like schizophrenia, where people lose touch with reality?", tag: "reason given, relatives named, term defined" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "There was an aunt they said had a nervous breakdown. She was put away for a while." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Tell me what you know about that." }
        ], note: "Composed. Repeat the sweep for the other side of the family. Three lessons from the book's own version: define technical words such as schizophrenia and bipolar disorder in plain language or they will be denied; ask separately whether anyone was ever hospitalized or institutionalized, because a concrete admission is remembered when a vague illness is not; and treat 'bad nerves' and 'nervous breakdown' as labels for serious illness that deserve follow-up. Ask about drinking and drugs by name, since patients may not count them as mental illness." },
        { type: "p", text: "Ask about suicide as its own question: has anyone in the family tried to end their life, or died that way. Patients who have just denied any serious family illness suddenly remember a suicide. Family history also grows on re-interview, and family members asked the same questions produce surprises; make the habit of asking them too." },
        { type: "p", text: "Cross-cultural care matters here as everywhere. All cultures hesitate to share relatives' psychiatric histories; for many Asian and Asian American patients mental illness in the family can approach taboo, so direct questions about mental illness fail. Michael Cheng suggests cueing off the patient's own symptoms: has anyone in the family had difficulties like yours, mood problems like yours, anxiety like yours, or been particularly stressed as you have been (then ask whether they had similar symptoms). Even 'nervous breakdown' may sound less stigmatizing than 'psychiatric illness,' and these indirect questions help any patient who fears stigma, whatever their background. Use the culture's own word for the depressive equivalent, asking a Latino/a patient whether any relative developed nervios under great stress, and remember that Asian, Hispanic, and many refugee populations express depression somatically. Some cultures also have disorders of their own (Chapter 12), or do not regard a familiar syndrome as psychiatric; a Vietnamese patient may not report a relative's trung gio, a panic-like attack attributed to the wind, with its own anticipatory avoidance on windy days, unless you ask about it by name." },
        { type: "p", text: "Finally, a family history can open onto family dynamics. A patient asked whether his brother had problems with depression or drugs answered with astonished sarcasm that the brother was spotless, everyone's favorite, the one with the better report cards; a brief excursion into that reaction told the interviewer more than any pedigree. Mr. Collier's formulation: persistent depressive disorder, early onset, moderately severe; V-codes for relationship distress with his spouse (couples therapy was recommended, and the marriage proved central to his depression) and for the slapping of his child; no personality disorder but possible narcissistic traits; chronic bronchitis from smoking." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "The child-abuse V-code", text: "When you record a V-code for parental child abuse, describe the exact behavior and your estimate of its severity in the body of the record; the code has legal consequences. Here both parents reported a single slap, and the interviewer would document that, return to the topic in later sessions as engagement deepened, and consult a supervisor about whether the child should be interviewed and whether local reporting requirements applied. In your own setting, know the law before the day you need it." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c9p6", label: "Practice: taking a family history" }, { kind: "quiz", id: "ch9", label: "Quiz: Chapter 9" }]
    }
  ],

  deeper: [
    { title: "Video Module 9.1", text: "Sensitively Uncovering the Symptoms of Major Depressive Disorder: expanded didactics and annotated interview excerpts showing the diagnostic expansion of a major depressive episode, the blended expansion of this chapter in action. Watch it alongside Video Module 2.1." },
    { title: "Video Module 9.2 (optional)", text: "Three learning segments, each with didactics and an interview demonstration, on expanding panic disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, and adult attention-deficit disorder, disorders the book has no room for in print. The interviewing principles are the same ones you have just studied." },
    { title: "Read in the book", text: "The full DSM-5 criteria boxes reprinted in the chapter (major depressive episode, persistent depressive disorder, manic and hypomanic episodes, and both mixed-features specifiers), the complete dialogue of the neurovegetative expansion with Shea's annotations, the library encounter with Mr. Matthews, the Griesinger passages on melancholia with destructive tendencies, and the five diagnostic summaries in Shea's three-heading format." },
    { title: "Chapters this one connects to", text: "Chapter 4 for stilted versus blended expansions; Chapter 5 for gentle assumption, behavioral incidents, and anchor questions; Chapter 6 for the wellness triad; Chapter 7 for the matrix and the healing matrix effect; Chapter 8 for immediacy in euphoric mania; Chapter 10 for the phenomenology of depression and engaging depressed patients and families; Chapter 11 for the order of symptoms in psychosis and for postpartum psychosis; Chapters 14 and 15 for the borderline look-alikes; Chapter 17 for suicide assessment." },
    { title: "Sources Shea draws on", text: "The DSM-5 and DSM-IV-TR; Carlat's The Psychiatric Interview (SIG E CAPS and time-related anchor questions); Fawcett on anxiety, panic, and suicide; Goodwin and Jamison's Manic-Depressive Illness; Kraepelin, Bleuler, and Griesinger on mixed states; Strakowski on broadening bipolar diagnosis; Smith on bipolar II; Klein on denial of depression; Cristancho, Nierenberg, and McGrath on atypical depression; Anderson's organic causes of depression; Nurnberger and the COGA study; Cheng's tips on family history with Asian American patients; Lewis-Fernández on culture and anxiety." }
  ]
};
